---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Nav from '../../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';
---

<Layout title="About | Ancient Chinese Architecture Project" description="Learn about Ancient Chinese Architecture, a digital museum exploring 3,000 years of Chinese architectural heritage through interactive tools and visual guides.">
  <Nav />
  <section class="pt-24 pb-16 px-6 bg-stone-light">
    <div class="max-w-[900px] mx-auto">
      <p class="text-vermillion text-sm font-medium tracking-widest uppercase mb-4 font-display">About</p>
      <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-display font-bold text-ink tracking-tight mb-6">About This Project</h1>
      <div class="prose prose-lg text-ink-muted space-y-4">
        <p>Ancient Chinese Architecture is a digital museum dedicated to exploring 3,000 years of Chinese architectural heritage. From the rammed-earth foundations of the Shang dynasty to the ornate palace complexes of the Qing, our mission is to make this extraordinary building tradition accessible, visual, and engaging.</p>
        <p>We believe that architecture tells the story of a civilization — its values, its technology, its relationship with nature and the cosmos. Every dougong bracket, every sweeping roof curve, every courtyard layout embodies centuries of accumulated wisdom and artistry.</p>
        <p>This site combines scholarly research with interactive tools and high-quality visuals to create an experience you cannot get from a textbook or a Wikipedia article. We are building the resource we wish had existed when we first became fascinated by Chinese architecture.</p>
        <h2 class="font-display font-bold text-2xl text-ink mt-8 mb-4">What This Site Covers</h2>
        <p>Our content is organized around four interconnected ways of understanding the tradition:</p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
          <li><strong>Twelve landmark buildings</strong> — from the 1,000-year-old Yingxian Pagoda and the Zhaozhou Bridge to the Forbidden City and the Ming Tombs. Each building page includes its construction history, engineering innovations, key dates, a practical travel guide, and answers to common questions.</li>
          <li><strong>Six core architectural elements</strong> — the dougong bracket system, roof forms, courtyard layouts, decorative systems, materials, and vernacular housing. These explain the grammar that every Chinese builder worked with, from imperial palaces to village homes.</li>
          <li><strong>Nine dynastic periods</strong> — from the rammed-earth cities of the Shang to the garden architecture of the Qing, showing how political power, religion, and technology reshaped building practice every few centuries.</li>
          <li><strong>Comparison tools and galleries</strong> — interactive side-by-side dynasty comparisons, a curated photo gallery, and visual guides that let you see the differences rather than just read about them.</li>
        </ul>
        <h2 class="font-display font-bold text-2xl text-ink mt-8 mb-4">Our Editorial Standards</h2>
        <p>Every page on this site is built from verifiable sources: archaeological excavation reports, conservation documents published by cultural heritage authorities, classical texts such as the <em>Yingzao Fashi</em> (Building Standards, 1103 CE), and current academic research in English and Chinese. Dates, measurements, and attributions are cross-checked against at least two independent sources before publication.</p>
        <p>We state uncertainty honestly. Where scholars disagree — for example, on the exact function of certain bracket configurations or the dating of particular tombs — we present the leading interpretations rather than a single oversimplified answer. When new excavations change our understanding, we revise the affected pages and note the update.</p>
        <p>Photographs on the site are credited and, wherever possible, taken from official sources or used under open licenses. We intentionally avoid AI-generated imagery for historical buildings because accuracy matters more than polish.</p>
        <h2 class="font-display font-bold text-2xl text-ink mt-8 mb-4">How to Use This Site</h2>
        <p>Start wherever your curiosity leads. If you are planning a trip, begin with the <a href="/buildings/" class="text-vermillion hover:text-vermillion-dark transition-colors">building collection</a> and use the travel guides on each page. If you want to understand why Chinese roofs curve upward or how brackets survive earthquakes, explore the <a href="/elements/" class="text-vermillion hover:text-vermillion-dark transition-colors">elements section</a>. For a broad sweep of history, the <a href="/dynasties/" class="text-vermillion hover:text-vermillion-dark transition-colors">dynasty timeline</a> provides the narrative spine, and the <a href="/compare/" class="text-vermillion hover:text-vermillion-dark transition-colors">comparison tool</a> lets you test your eye for stylistic differences. The <a href="/gallery/" class="text-vermillion hover:text-vermillion-dark transition-colors">gallery</a> works best as a visual index — find a building you love and jump to its full page.</p>
        <h2 id="contact" class="font-display font-bold text-2xl text-ink mt-8 mb-4">Contact</h2>
        <p>For inquiries, corrections, or contributions, reach us at <a href="mailto:hello@ancient-chinese-architecture.com" class="text-vermillion hover:text-vermillion-dark transition-colors">hello@ancient-chinese-architecture.com</a>.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <Footer />
</Layout>
